Steve,

Thanks for your information and confirmation of the modeling approach that I
should use.

I guess I am still wondering, if my models DO converge without the stepwise
approach and setting starting values manually (p. 119 of User guide), is it
still necessary to use the nested/stepwise approach?  I have seen other papers
that do as I have and select models a priori with "all combinations of up to 2
additional covariates" (e.g. Forcada et al. 2004, MEPS 275: 275-287).
The User
manual seems to recommend the stepwise approach as a solution to models
that do
NOT converge.

It is still a little unclear to me what exactly makes a nested modeling
approach
in DISTANCE.  Does it become a nested analysis when you set starting values
manually based on the previous model?  I can't find anything in the User Guide
that refers explicitly to nested modeling in MCDS.

Regardless if the stepwise approach should be used or not, it seems to me that
different results may arise depending on the order of the stepwise addition of
covariates.  Consider that I am fitting the following 4 covariates: Year (YR),
Observer (OBS), cluster size (CS), and seastate (SS).  The guide suggests
starting with the covariate that gives the best model (say OBS) then
adding one
additional covariate at a time, until there is NO decrease in the AIC.  Could
you not end up with a different final model depending on the order of
addition:
OBS+YR+CS+SS vs. OBS+SS+CS+YR?  What is a correct order for addition of
covariates in forward stepwise?

Thanks,

Rob

